WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.035
&gt;&gt; It's great to be here
today with you, Dario.

00:00:10.036 --> 00:00:13.290
It's great having a Director
of Safety with us.

00:00:13.291 --> 00:00:17.600
In this case, Director of
Safety of AI Systems in OpenAI.

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:19.725
Thanks for joining us today and

00:00:19.726 --> 00:00:21.240
just give a fabulous lecture,

00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:24.715
and folks out there who
see this fireside chat,

00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:26.490
who have not senior lecturer yet,

00:00:26.491 --> 00:00:29.020
this is a pointer to the lecture.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:33.680
What remarkable is in terms

00:00:33.681 --> 00:00:37.390
of similarity between us is
that we've had similar pasts,

00:00:37.391 --> 00:00:39.500
just talk a little bit about how

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:40.670
you got to where you are now.

00:00:40.671 --> 00:00:43.430
It's an interesting trajectory
towards the world of

00:00:43.431 --> 00:00:44.960
intelligence and
into their notion

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:47.300
of onto safety and robustness.

00:00:47.301 --> 00:00:49.600
But you started out in physics,

00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:51.980
and tell me a bit

00:00:51.981 --> 00:00:53.640
about how that progressed
to where you are?

00:00:53.641 --> 00:00:57.315
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I mean, I
think when I was younger,

00:00:57.316 --> 00:01:01.050
I really wanted to figure
out how the universe works,

00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:02.530
why I wanted to be
a physicist wanting

00:01:02.531 --> 00:01:05.995
to drive fundamental laws.

00:01:05.996 --> 00:01:09.090
Then so I did
my undergrad in that.

00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:10.670
When I went to grad school,

00:01:10.671 --> 00:01:12.285
which was also in physics,

00:01:12.286 --> 00:01:16.810
I got really interested
in what can,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:21.140
both the methods and the
recording devices from physics,

00:01:21.141 --> 00:01:22.940
tell us about neuroscience

00:01:22.941 --> 00:01:24.425
in the brain and in intelligence.

00:01:24.426 --> 00:01:27.590
I thought of doing
AI at that time,

00:01:27.591 --> 00:01:29.210
but I felt like I really wanted

00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:31.040
to understand the brain because

00:01:31.041 --> 00:01:34.010
it's the only existing example

00:01:34.011 --> 00:01:36.335
of a truly intelligent system.

00:01:36.336 --> 00:01:38.870
So, I spent a fair
amount of time doing

00:01:38.871 --> 00:01:41.360
that in grad school,

00:01:41.361 --> 00:01:46.045
did kind of a postdoc in
computational biology,

00:01:46.046 --> 00:01:47.935
and then through Stanford,

00:01:47.936 --> 00:01:49.725
got to know about

00:01:49.726 --> 00:01:52.160
speech research project going on

00:01:52.161 --> 00:01:53.900
at Baidu under rendering.

00:01:53.901 --> 00:01:57.660
So I was there, then I
was at Google for a year,

00:01:57.661 --> 00:02:01.340
and a little over a couple
years ago, I joined OpenAI,

00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:03.590
and sometime around
where I was working

00:02:03.591 --> 00:02:06.505
on things at Baidu and Google,

00:02:06.506 --> 00:02:08.180
just got a lot of experience

00:02:08.181 --> 00:02:11.610
with the brittleness
of neural nets.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.775
In one sense, they're
very robust and powerful.

00:02:15.776 --> 00:02:19.819
But that when you go off
some given distribution,

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:22.175
they don't do so well and

00:02:22.176 --> 00:02:26.220
that it's hard to

00:02:26.221 --> 00:02:28.950
understand really what's
going on inside them,

00:02:28.951 --> 00:02:30.845
and particularly that
in the long run as

00:02:30.846 --> 00:02:32.890
we make systems more powerful.

00:02:32.891 --> 00:02:34.970
These problems are only
going to get worse,

00:02:34.971 --> 00:02:37.360
so I decided that was
what I wanted to work on.

00:02:37.361 --> 00:02:39.950
&gt;&gt; But going back, it
will come back to where

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:41.210
you are now in the great research

00:02:41.211 --> 00:02:43.155
that you're doing with your team,

00:02:43.156 --> 00:02:46.880
but going back to
this decision or

00:02:46.881 --> 00:02:49.185
this deep curiosity about

00:02:49.186 --> 00:02:52.134
what's going on in
brains and neurobiology,

00:02:52.135 --> 00:02:54.185
what's the spark of that?

00:02:54.186 --> 00:02:56.115
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I mean,

00:02:56.116 --> 00:02:58.775
when I got to Princeton
for grad school,

00:02:58.776 --> 00:03:03.320
there were a few biophysicists
there working on

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.450
models of neural circuits
based on statistical mechanics

00:03:08.451 --> 00:03:09.860
and having done a fair amount of

00:03:09.861 --> 00:03:11.990
statistical mechanics
as a physicist.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:17.450
I was really interested
in what can we say

00:03:17.451 --> 00:03:20.330
with these kind of
statistical models

00:03:20.331 --> 00:03:23.030
that are based on
thermodynamics about the brain,

00:03:23.031 --> 00:03:24.770
can it give us any insight.

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:27.440
In vague terms, a lot of

00:03:27.441 --> 00:03:29.730
people were analyzing
single neuron saying,

00:03:29.731 --> 00:03:31.885
"Here's a circuit,
this neuron does this,

00:03:31.886 --> 00:03:33.340
that neuron does this."

00:03:33.341 --> 00:03:35.480
Maybe things are more
about the population and

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:36.710
the statistical properties and

00:03:36.711 --> 00:03:38.470
that seem very intuitive to me,

00:03:38.471 --> 00:03:43.970
so I kind of wanted to devote
myself to looking into it.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.580
&gt;&gt; Do you feel like in
advance of your work in AI,

00:03:48.581 --> 00:03:51.920
you came away with
any intuitions or

00:03:51.921 --> 00:03:53.525
insights about what
the heck's going

00:03:53.526 --> 00:03:55.530
on in vertebrate brains?

00:03:55.531 --> 00:03:55.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:58.310
&gt;&gt; If any brains will throw
invertebrates in there too?

00:03:58.311 --> 00:04:00.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah. It's hard.

00:04:00.421 --> 00:04:01.630
I mean, I think
one lesson I learned

00:04:01.631 --> 00:04:03.190
about the field of
neuroscience is

00:04:03.191 --> 00:04:04.780
that it's very limited by

00:04:04.781 --> 00:04:06.690
our ability to measure
what's going on,

00:04:06.691 --> 00:04:09.755
and the brain theory
is overdetermined.

00:04:09.756 --> 00:04:13.160
It's actually very, very much
like in particle physics,

00:04:13.161 --> 00:04:15.550
where you can come up with
a whole bunch of theories,

00:04:15.551 --> 00:04:17.380
but if you only have
a little bit of data,

00:04:17.381 --> 00:04:18.850
then there are too many theories

00:04:18.851 --> 00:04:20.260
that could fit that data.

00:04:20.261 --> 00:04:21.610
So, in neuroscience, we're

00:04:21.611 --> 00:04:24.450
always trying to get more data.

00:04:24.451 --> 00:04:27.750
I spent a lot of
my PhD trying to do

00:04:27.751 --> 00:04:30.930
that with at least some success,

00:04:30.931 --> 00:04:35.150
although much less than
I would have hoped

00:04:35.151 --> 00:04:37.160
for than I think the field needs

00:04:37.161 --> 00:04:40.920
to really understand things.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.180
I did get some instincts that

00:04:45.181 --> 00:04:49.510
things like simple reinforcement
learning approaches,

00:04:49.511 --> 00:04:52.065
maybe in some ways
on the right track.

00:04:52.066 --> 00:04:55.325
If you look at hippocampus,

00:04:55.326 --> 00:04:57.260
there are things that look like

00:04:57.261 --> 00:04:59.030
temporal difference
policy updates

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:02.330
happening that looks
surprisingly much like it.

00:05:02.331 --> 00:05:05.420
So, the idea that
their RL is a piece,

00:05:05.421 --> 00:05:08.840
which really has ended up
being right in the field,

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:12.695
it was like one of
the lessons that I drew.

00:05:12.696 --> 00:05:16.430
&gt;&gt; So, it was great reading
about your earlier work

00:05:16.431 --> 00:05:21.095
and the work he did during
grad school in neurobiology.

00:05:21.096 --> 00:05:23.435
It was fun to talk
to you over lunch,

00:05:23.436 --> 00:05:26.695
just now where we compared
some notes in that.

00:05:26.696 --> 00:05:30.020
At Microsoft Research, we
have had a collaboration with

00:05:30.021 --> 00:05:32.194
Bill Kristan's team at UCSD

00:05:32.195 --> 00:05:34.460
taking the latest in ML tools,

00:05:34.461 --> 00:05:36.650
machine learning
and visualization,

00:05:36.651 --> 00:05:38.180
to try to understand what's going

00:05:38.181 --> 00:05:40.445
on with sets of neurons,

00:05:40.446 --> 00:05:42.840
in this case, in
the medicinal leech

00:05:42.841 --> 00:05:44.940
and you working in salamander.

00:05:44.941 --> 00:05:47.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, yeah.

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:50.480
I've done some work
on salamander retina,

00:05:50.481 --> 00:05:54.920
and really just the logic
there was to try and

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:58.535
understand the nervous system

00:05:58.536 --> 00:06:02.320
of a complex animal by taking
a separable piece of it,

00:06:02.321 --> 00:06:04.275
where the salamander retina,

00:06:04.276 --> 00:06:06.320
you can fully control what

00:06:06.321 --> 00:06:08.540
goes into the salamander retina,

00:06:08.541 --> 00:06:10.505
and then look at what
goes up the optic nerve.

00:06:10.506 --> 00:06:12.499
So, you have this kind of SandBox

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.050
that's an isolated piece
of the brain.

00:06:14.051 --> 00:06:16.795
You can control the inputs,
observe the outputs,

00:06:16.796 --> 00:06:19.955
and look at what's happening
to every neuron in-between.

00:06:19.956 --> 00:06:21.830
What we focused on was trying to

00:06:21.831 --> 00:06:24.320
record from a large number
of these neurons and saying,

00:06:24.321 --> 00:06:26.640
"What is the whole population
of neurons doing?

00:06:26.641 --> 00:06:29.215
What is it coding for?

00:06:29.216 --> 00:06:32.680
How does it collectively
communicate information?"

00:06:32.681 --> 00:06:34.580
That got me thinking
about these kind of

00:06:34.581 --> 00:06:37.175
higher-level questions
about intelligence.

00:06:37.176 --> 00:06:43.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I just personally
been blown away by what

00:06:43.071 --> 00:06:46.940
evolutionary biology
discovered they could do with

00:06:46.941 --> 00:06:50.980
cells and tangles of
cells to do cognition,

00:06:50.981 --> 00:06:53.140
whether it be invertebrates
or vertebrates.

00:06:53.141 --> 00:06:56.280
To me, I would certainly
have stuck with

00:06:56.281 --> 00:06:59.225
neurobiology if I thought I
can make better progress.

00:06:59.226 --> 00:07:00.535
I don't know if you feel
the same way or not.

00:07:00.536 --> 00:07:02.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I feel the same way.

00:07:02.211 --> 00:07:05.820
I mean, we're very
much limited by data.

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:10.700
When we have a very limited
amount of data,

00:07:10.701 --> 00:07:11.840
then you can come
up with all kinds

00:07:11.841 --> 00:07:13.070
of theories for it and

00:07:13.071 --> 00:07:14.630
it's overdetermined to many

00:07:14.631 --> 00:07:15.910
of your theories fit the data.

00:07:15.911 --> 00:07:17.940
So, we're always trying
to collect more data,

00:07:17.941 --> 00:07:20.870
and I wanted to make these
kind of theories and models

00:07:20.871 --> 00:07:24.290
but spent most of my time
improving the data,

00:07:24.291 --> 00:07:27.235
and I had, I think,
some success with that.

00:07:27.236 --> 00:07:32.400
But if I felt that we
were really at the point

00:07:32.401 --> 00:07:34.620
where we could record

00:07:34.621 --> 00:07:36.810
millions of neurons,
pour through the data,

00:07:36.811 --> 00:07:39.255
and try and analyze it,

00:07:39.256 --> 00:07:41.655
and we knew the data
was accurate,

00:07:41.656 --> 00:07:44.180
and we could correlate
it to stimuli

00:07:44.181 --> 00:07:46.980
in human behavior and
the thoughts in humans had,

00:07:46.981 --> 00:07:48.680
then we could really

00:07:48.681 --> 00:07:51.190
have a mature science
of neuroscience.

00:07:51.191 --> 00:07:52.910
Probably, if that was the case,

00:07:52.911 --> 00:07:54.170
then I would have
stayed in the field.

00:07:54.171 --> 00:07:56.345
But I really felt we were limited

00:07:56.346 --> 00:07:59.505
in what we could do and what
we could record in that.

00:07:59.506 --> 00:08:01.340
Actually, AI was answering

00:08:01.341 --> 00:08:03.050
our questions in
more interesting ways.

00:08:03.051 --> 00:08:05.065
&gt;&gt; So, I think for
people like you and I,

00:08:05.066 --> 00:08:07.360
AI is our neuroscience right now.

00:08:07.361 --> 00:08:09.675
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I would say so.

00:08:09.676 --> 00:08:12.720
&gt;&gt; Moving into your work

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:17.830
to an AI company
going from a postdoc,

00:08:17.831 --> 00:08:18.920
I guess into Baidu,

00:08:18.921 --> 00:08:20.085
is going to distinct the jump.

00:08:20.086 --> 00:08:20.280
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:08:20.281 --> 00:08:21.735
&gt;&gt; How did that happen exactly?

00:08:21.736 --> 00:08:24.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I mean very

00:08:24.061 --> 00:08:26.435
much kind of just as
you just mentioned,

00:08:26.436 --> 00:08:28.205
I ended up picking up

00:08:28.206 --> 00:08:30.290
a fair amount of ML
along the way when

00:08:30.291 --> 00:08:32.210
we were doing like spike signal

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:33.770
sorting for the neural arrays,

00:08:33.771 --> 00:08:35.510
or just as you mentioned,

00:08:35.511 --> 00:08:37.760
just the pure signal
processing tasks,

00:08:37.761 --> 00:08:39.935
never even mind the neuroscience,

00:08:39.936 --> 00:08:41.765
ends up using a lot of

00:08:41.766 --> 00:08:43.560
machine learning and
pattern recognition.

00:08:43.561 --> 00:08:48.545
So, I would say it was like
an amateur in the field,

00:08:48.546 --> 00:08:51.910
but not someone who didn't
know no anything about it.

00:08:51.911 --> 00:08:55.450
Then when I found out about
this effort at Baidu,

00:08:55.451 --> 00:08:57.860
it's kind of just as it
was getting started.

00:08:57.861 --> 00:09:01.905
I felt like it was
really an opportunity,

00:09:01.906 --> 00:09:04.095
so I went away and

00:09:04.096 --> 00:09:07.100
studied convolutional neural nets

00:09:07.101 --> 00:09:08.330
and recurrent neural nets

00:09:08.331 --> 00:09:11.930
for awhile and ended up getting

00:09:11.931 --> 00:09:16.835
hired and I really enjoyed
it and never looked back.

00:09:16.836 --> 00:09:20.350
&gt;&gt; In your work on
speech for the leaps

00:09:20.351 --> 00:09:23.865
that you're able to push out,

00:09:23.866 --> 00:09:26.470
what were some of
the techniques and insights

00:09:26.471 --> 00:09:27.850
that helped you out

00:09:27.851 --> 00:09:29.785
there in terms of getting
those new results?

00:09:29.786 --> 00:09:32.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, fair
amount of it was

00:09:32.591 --> 00:09:35.440
just kind of pure
scale and engineering,

00:09:35.441 --> 00:09:38.240
so I was more in
the ML side but also worked

00:09:38.241 --> 00:09:41.740
with the systems folks.

00:09:41.741 --> 00:09:43.870
So, I think one of
the things that

00:09:43.871 --> 00:09:46.630
the Baidu lab was successful

00:09:46.631 --> 00:09:50.570
at was using Synchronous
Stochastic Gradient Descent

00:09:50.571 --> 00:09:52.775
before other people did.

00:09:52.776 --> 00:09:54.615
&gt;&gt; Can you describe
what that means?

00:09:54.616 --> 00:09:56.789
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, basically,

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:59.015
the standard, the way,

00:09:59.016 --> 00:10:01.585
kind of, AI result first
started coming out of

00:10:01.586 --> 00:10:04.125
Google in Toronto in 2012.

00:10:04.126 --> 00:10:08.690
The standard thing was
Asynchronous Gradient Descent.

00:10:08.691 --> 00:10:11.000
So, this was done within
Google very much kind of like

00:10:11.001 --> 00:10:14.040
the web server model
where each machine

00:10:14.041 --> 00:10:17.510
has a batch and each machine
computes the gradient to

00:10:17.511 --> 00:10:19.370
that batch and there's
some central server

00:10:19.371 --> 00:10:21.520
that has the value of
all the parameters.

00:10:21.521 --> 00:10:22.245
&gt;&gt; Parameters server.

00:10:22.246 --> 00:10:23.280
&gt;&gt; Yeah, parameter server.

00:10:23.281 --> 00:10:24.995
The gradients get applied and

00:10:24.996 --> 00:10:26.940
so it's asynchronous
because I can be

00:10:26.941 --> 00:10:29.380
calculating the gradients of
one model and apply it to

00:10:29.381 --> 00:10:32.030
some new model that's been
updated in the meantime.

00:10:32.031 --> 00:10:34.180
Synchronous gradient
descent is more like,

00:10:34.181 --> 00:10:35.595
I have all these machines,

00:10:35.596 --> 00:10:38.205
they compute gradients,
they do a Big-O reduce,

00:10:38.206 --> 00:10:39.620
sum up all the gradients and

00:10:39.621 --> 00:10:41.030
apply that gradient, everything.

00:10:41.031 --> 00:10:44.570
So, the whole set of
machines takes one big step,

00:10:44.571 --> 00:10:47.085
and then one big step,
and then one big step.

00:10:47.086 --> 00:10:50.240
That turns out to have
benefits in terms of kind of

00:10:50.241 --> 00:10:53.100
reproducibility and also seems

00:10:53.101 --> 00:10:54.890
to in some contexts
lead to better results.

00:10:54.891 --> 00:10:56.040
&gt;&gt; Do you think part of that

00:10:56.041 --> 00:10:59.260
was the advancing
computation at the time?

00:10:59.261 --> 00:11:02.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That's about what
I was about to get to.

00:11:02.021 --> 00:11:03.910
A lot of the stuff we did in

00:11:03.911 --> 00:11:08.785
speech was just our ability
to kind of scale things up,

00:11:08.786 --> 00:11:11.420
use more compute,
collect more data.

00:11:11.421 --> 00:11:12.730
We even kind of made

00:11:12.731 --> 00:11:14.900
these graphs of how
much better do you

00:11:14.901 --> 00:11:16.650
do as a result of number

00:11:16.651 --> 00:11:18.560
of parameters if
the architecture is right,

00:11:18.561 --> 00:11:22.245
number of- the
amount of amount of

00:11:22.246 --> 00:11:23.965
data that you use and you see

00:11:23.966 --> 00:11:26.560
these very smooth log plots.

00:11:26.561 --> 00:11:29.605
After I left Baidu, they
explored this some more and it

00:11:29.606 --> 00:11:32.500
turned into this paper called
Deep Learning Training.

00:11:32.501 --> 00:11:34.400
Deep learning scaling
is predictable

00:11:34.401 --> 00:11:36.095
empirically and that's been

00:11:36.096 --> 00:11:37.970
one thing that's
shaped my thinking

00:11:37.971 --> 00:11:40.115
that kind of once you know,

00:11:40.116 --> 00:11:42.640
once you have the paradigm

00:11:42.641 --> 00:11:44.660
for how to solve
a particular task.

00:11:44.661 --> 00:11:47.285
Once you have all the
conceptual stuff right

00:11:47.286 --> 00:11:50.515
then the scaling has the super
smooth properties to it.

00:11:50.516 --> 00:11:55.115
&gt;&gt; Now, it's clear that there's

00:11:55.116 --> 00:12:00.995
been for a number of
years now, excitement,

00:12:00.996 --> 00:12:04.560
exuberance, about
the possibilities

00:12:04.561 --> 00:12:10.430
of breakthroughs in AI
also fears, anxieties,

00:12:10.431 --> 00:12:14.200
and these different opinions

00:12:14.201 --> 00:12:16.260
on which way things might
go and whether there

00:12:16.261 --> 00:12:18.825
are deep concerns to be

00:12:18.826 --> 00:12:22.394
grappled with or it's
time to celebrate

00:12:22.395 --> 00:12:26.520
immortality through the advent of

00:12:26.521 --> 00:12:28.875
powerful machinery that we

00:12:28.876 --> 00:12:31.825
can jump into some day
for some people

00:12:31.826 --> 00:12:34.160
have captured the attention of

00:12:34.161 --> 00:12:36.830
the public and
some technologists and

00:12:36.831 --> 00:12:38.300
some scientists and I'm

00:12:38.301 --> 00:12:41.020
curious if that was

00:12:41.021 --> 00:12:42.905
the context in which you
start working in safety.

00:12:42.906 --> 00:12:44.245
Well, how did you get
going on safety front?

00:12:44.246 --> 00:12:50.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I think I was
kind of aware of some of

00:12:50.381 --> 00:12:56.980
the kind of more extreme views
in terms of safety.

00:12:56.981 --> 00:12:59.910
In many cases, I
thought people were,

00:12:59.911 --> 00:13:02.355
it was true kind of like jump
ahead of themselves

00:13:02.356 --> 00:13:06.310
or not thinking about
the problem in the right way.

00:13:06.311 --> 00:13:07.540
But at the same time,

00:13:07.541 --> 00:13:09.580
I thought that there
was really a kernel

00:13:09.581 --> 00:13:11.660
of important truth to

00:13:11.661 --> 00:13:13.390
some of what they
were saying which is

00:13:13.391 --> 00:13:16.050
that if you have
a powerful AI system,

00:13:16.051 --> 00:13:17.225
and today we have

00:13:17.226 --> 00:13:18.995
somewhat powerful
AI systems but they're

00:13:18.996 --> 00:13:21.990
getting more and more
powerful every day.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:26.550
If that system does
has goals, objectives,

00:13:26.551 --> 00:13:28.550
that are not aligned with
the ones you want them to be,

00:13:28.551 --> 00:13:36.305
that can lead to accidents,
to catastrophic behavior.

00:13:36.306 --> 00:13:41.365
My perspective on it,
the perspective that

00:13:41.366 --> 00:13:45.880
I hope I've helped to kind
of bring to the safety field

00:13:45.881 --> 00:13:47.845
is this empirical perspective of

00:13:47.846 --> 00:13:50.545
let's look at today's systems,

00:13:50.546 --> 00:13:52.730
if you think of some problem

00:13:52.731 --> 00:13:54.560
that you might have
in the future,

00:13:54.561 --> 00:13:56.000
some safety issue
that you might have

00:13:56.001 --> 00:13:58.270
with future powerful AI systems,

00:13:58.271 --> 00:14:00.610
can you kind of build
a replica of that

00:14:00.611 --> 00:14:03.530
today in today's systems to study

00:14:03.531 --> 00:14:05.990
the problems conceptually
and build kind of

00:14:05.991 --> 00:14:07.660
durable safety
approaches so that if

00:14:07.661 --> 00:14:09.530
the AI system gets more powerful,

00:14:09.531 --> 00:14:10.930
the safety approach gets

00:14:10.931 --> 00:14:13.930
powerful along with
it and someday we'll

00:14:13.931 --> 00:14:18.555
have super intelligent machines
and then I hope that day,

00:14:18.556 --> 00:14:20.460
that on that day
we've been spending

00:14:20.461 --> 00:14:23.770
many years making the safety
systems better and better.

00:14:23.771 --> 00:14:26.800
So, it's a problem that
when we finally do face it,

00:14:26.801 --> 00:14:28.855
we do know how to
grapple with it.

00:14:28.856 --> 00:14:33.340
&gt;&gt; I think it's, just to
reflect a bit in real-time,

00:14:33.341 --> 00:14:38.640
there are different often
unstated intuitions

00:14:38.641 --> 00:14:42.400
about what the concerns
are notions of something

00:14:42.401 --> 00:14:43.855
might go wrong and there's

00:14:43.856 --> 00:14:46.500
some costly outcome but
it's noticed and seen

00:14:46.501 --> 00:14:48.280
and people set the system down or

00:14:48.281 --> 00:14:51.510
they addressed an issue and
make the system better.

00:14:51.511 --> 00:14:53.780
There's the idea of something big

00:14:53.781 --> 00:14:56.575
happens and it's hard
to ever stop it and

00:14:56.576 --> 00:15:00.055
it's a long-term oppression

00:15:00.056 --> 00:15:03.510
of human beings and
there's a third notion of,

00:15:03.511 --> 00:15:06.390
many more, of something is going

00:15:06.391 --> 00:15:09.980
wrong under the hood
and implicitly it's

00:15:09.981 --> 00:15:12.605
creating some nuances
in the world that

00:15:12.606 --> 00:15:17.345
make us unhappy as
individuals or is

00:15:17.346 --> 00:15:21.105
costly to the society
like even how automation

00:15:21.106 --> 00:15:26.085
might shift cause more inequity
in wealth for example.

00:15:26.086 --> 00:15:28.230
I'm curious which sort
of where you think

00:15:28.231 --> 00:15:30.820
you're concerned, I mean
what you're addressing?

00:15:30.821 --> 00:15:35.040
&gt;&gt; All of the above to
different degrees, right?

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:37.970
So, I think there could be kind

00:15:37.971 --> 00:15:40.375
of unitary problems or rebuild

00:15:40.376 --> 00:15:42.890
a specific AI system
where we wanted

00:15:42.891 --> 00:15:45.575
to do X and then instead does Y.

00:15:45.576 --> 00:15:46.770
&gt;&gt; But that's noticed?

00:15:46.771 --> 00:15:48.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and it's noticed

00:15:48.441 --> 00:15:50.280
but maybe something
catastrophic happens

00:15:50.281 --> 00:15:55.615
before we notice it and we
corrected but damage was done,

00:15:55.616 --> 00:15:57.750
I mean, this is the case
in self-driving cars.

00:15:57.751 --> 00:16:00.900
They have problems,
people crashes,

00:16:00.901 --> 00:16:04.110
eventually some people
will die, already have.

00:16:04.111 --> 00:16:08.220
Then if we increase the stakes
to systems that are

00:16:08.221 --> 00:16:12.390
making important decisions
for the economy,

00:16:12.391 --> 00:16:14.865
the financial system,
humanity as a whole,

00:16:14.866 --> 00:16:18.650
these kind of discrete danger
certainly can happen.

00:16:18.651 --> 00:16:20.565
At the same time, there's kind of

00:16:20.566 --> 00:16:24.645
like diffusion throughout
the economy of

00:16:24.646 --> 00:16:30.040
AI systems like subtly
changing society in some way,

00:16:30.041 --> 00:16:31.554
or changing the economy,

00:16:31.555 --> 00:16:34.640
or changing how humans
relate to each other,

00:16:34.641 --> 00:16:37.605
in a way that's like
could be really

00:16:37.606 --> 00:16:39.280
bad but that we don't
notice right away.

00:16:39.281 --> 00:16:41.180
I mean I'm very- I think there

00:16:41.181 --> 00:16:43.230
are opportunities
for that as well.

00:16:43.231 --> 00:16:44.830
Of course, there are
also opportunities

00:16:44.831 --> 00:16:48.330
for AI to have
positive effects of

00:16:48.331 --> 00:16:54.365
that type to help humans
make better decisions,

00:16:54.366 --> 00:16:55.650
to understand themselves better,

00:16:55.651 --> 00:16:58.570
to do all kinds of
things like that.

00:16:58.571 --> 00:17:02.235
So, I see both the
positive and the negative.

00:17:02.236 --> 00:17:04.270
&gt;&gt; In the paper, you
wrote a few years ago.

00:17:04.271 --> 00:17:07.665
I think this was, maybe
you're coming out,

00:17:07.666 --> 00:17:11.140
manuscript that
you will be taking

00:17:11.141 --> 00:17:15.135
a serious look at
safety issues as a co-author.

00:17:15.136 --> 00:17:19.965
You mentioned five concrete
types of problems you saw.

00:17:19.966 --> 00:17:21.880
I think you had mentioned
and talked a little

00:17:21.881 --> 00:17:24.100
bit about these concerns.

00:17:24.101 --> 00:17:27.420
&gt;&gt; So, the one that
people ended up,

00:17:27.421 --> 00:17:30.740
liking the most was
reward hacking.

00:17:30.741 --> 00:17:34.150
So, it's this idea that you're
trying to get an AI system

00:17:34.151 --> 00:17:36.910
to do something complicated like,

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:44.480
some complicated human
activity like just some,

00:17:44.481 --> 00:17:46.590
I can't think of an example
off the top my head,

00:17:46.591 --> 00:17:49.640
but you have some simple
proxy, I don't know.

00:17:49.641 --> 00:17:52.295
An example would be like
trying to help humans

00:17:52.296 --> 00:17:53.660
connect to each other
better or something

00:17:53.661 --> 00:17:55.465
like that or make humans happier.

00:17:55.466 --> 00:17:57.200
You have some simple
proxies that are

00:17:57.201 --> 00:17:59.120
usually correlated
with it and you

00:17:59.121 --> 00:18:01.660
start to optimize one of
the proxies and as you

00:18:01.661 --> 00:18:04.590
do more and more
optimizing of the proxy,

00:18:04.591 --> 00:18:05.775
it kind of starts to diverge in

00:18:05.776 --> 00:18:08.020
the thing you're
ultimately trying to get.

00:18:08.021 --> 00:18:11.945
Like a lot of tech products,

00:18:11.946 --> 00:18:14.305
it's really easy to
measure like engagement

00:18:14.306 --> 00:18:16.530
or clicks and they're

00:18:16.531 --> 00:18:18.855
kind of used as
a lazy proxy for the user

00:18:18.856 --> 00:18:21.240
is getting what they want and
users enriching themselves.

00:18:21.241 --> 00:18:23.300
That's maybe true before

00:18:23.301 --> 00:18:25.595
you optimize heavily for
it but once you optimize

00:18:25.596 --> 00:18:27.615
heavily for it that
correlation can break and

00:18:27.616 --> 00:18:30.150
so noting that this
is a general problem

00:18:30.151 --> 00:18:31.930
for Reinforcement
Learning Systems

00:18:31.931 --> 00:18:34.720
and whatever comes after
Reinforcement Learning Systems,

00:18:34.721 --> 00:18:39.465
RL Plus Plus, it's
a general feature of systems

00:18:39.466 --> 00:18:41.095
that are throwing a lot of

00:18:41.096 --> 00:18:44.670
optimization power and compute
to pursue a unitary goal.

00:18:44.671 --> 00:18:49.625
&gt;&gt; You showed
a really expressive video

00:18:49.626 --> 00:18:53.105
of what one of your systems
did when it discovered

00:18:53.106 --> 00:18:55.105
how to hack a value function

00:18:55.106 --> 00:18:57.085
or objective I should
say more generally.

00:18:57.086 --> 00:19:00.475
We can describe that game
and the result.

00:19:00.476 --> 00:19:02.485
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, it's
this boat race game.

00:19:02.486 --> 00:19:05.375
I probably shown this video
a bunch of times.

00:19:05.376 --> 00:19:06.865
&gt;&gt; The people haven't
seen that you just sort

00:19:06.866 --> 00:19:08.870
of click on that link,
they'll find it somewhere.

00:19:08.871 --> 00:19:10.325
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so it's like this boat

00:19:10.326 --> 00:19:11.380
is supposed to be completing

00:19:11.381 --> 00:19:15.795
this boat race and
the way it's setup is,

00:19:15.796 --> 00:19:19.180
I was training this among
hundreds of other games.

00:19:19.181 --> 00:19:20.575
So, I didn't look too closely at

00:19:20.576 --> 00:19:21.940
the reward function but

00:19:21.941 --> 00:19:23.530
the way it's setup
within the game is

00:19:23.531 --> 00:19:27.430
you get points for hitting
these targets along the along

00:19:27.431 --> 00:19:29.460
the course and then

00:19:29.461 --> 00:19:31.570
points for finishing the race
and so you would think,

00:19:31.571 --> 00:19:33.430
well that incentivizes, it's not

00:19:33.431 --> 00:19:35.440
quite the same as saying
finished the race,

00:19:35.441 --> 00:19:36.755
but you would think
it would incentivize

00:19:36.756 --> 00:19:38.015
the agent to finish the race.

00:19:38.016 --> 00:19:40.120
But it turns out they're
both confined to

00:19:40.121 --> 00:19:42.740
this isolated lagoon
and basically spin

00:19:42.741 --> 00:19:45.315
around in this perfectly
designed circle in order

00:19:45.316 --> 00:19:48.225
to keep getting these power
ups just as they regenerate.

00:19:48.226 --> 00:19:50.500
It can get such a high density

00:19:50.501 --> 00:19:51.855
of points that it's like I don't

00:19:51.856 --> 00:19:53.565
even need to finish the race
I'm just going to find

00:19:53.566 --> 00:19:55.590
this lagoon and spin
around in it forever.

00:19:55.591 --> 00:19:59.105
&gt;&gt; It's certainly showing in
that video is a way to you

00:19:59.106 --> 00:20:01.255
can save 30 minutes
of a presentation

00:20:01.256 --> 00:20:02.830
to say, "Look at this video."

00:20:02.831 --> 00:20:05.075
Here's what happened
with these methods

00:20:05.076 --> 00:20:09.390
to push the point
through very clearly.

00:20:09.391 --> 00:20:14.820
Other of these challenges
to AI systems in

00:20:14.821 --> 00:20:18.755
the open world include
notions that I would say are

00:20:18.756 --> 00:20:23.060
captured by the classic
frame problem in AI.

00:20:23.061 --> 00:20:23.315
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:20:23.316 --> 00:20:24.540
&gt;&gt; Let's talk about the notion

00:20:24.541 --> 00:20:27.830
that AI systems really didn't

00:20:27.831 --> 00:20:29.250
have enough knowledge
to understand

00:20:29.251 --> 00:20:32.450
the current state,
the qualification problem.

00:20:32.451 --> 00:20:34.040
When they took an
action in the world,

00:20:34.041 --> 00:20:37.670
they really didn't understand
the ramifications,

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.905
given how much knowledge
you would need to

00:20:40.906 --> 00:20:44.880
understand all the possible
effects of action.

00:20:44.881 --> 00:20:47.265
I get the sense that, that

00:20:47.266 --> 00:20:49.050
could characterize a lot
of what we're seeing

00:20:49.051 --> 00:20:50.820
now in modern AI

00:20:50.821 --> 00:20:53.570
as we work with
these probabilistic systems.

00:20:53.571 --> 00:20:55.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah. No, I think
it's very true I mean

00:20:55.891 --> 00:20:59.005
it's what I had in mind with
some of the safety problems.

00:20:59.006 --> 00:21:01.640
That the world is
a really big place

00:21:01.641 --> 00:21:05.440
and agents or agent brains
are relatively small,

00:21:05.441 --> 00:21:07.220
human's brains are
a relatively small.

00:21:07.221 --> 00:21:08.530
There's much more in
the world that can

00:21:08.531 --> 00:21:11.060
ever fit in your head.

00:21:11.061 --> 00:21:13.710
Also at least for our
reinforcement learning agents,

00:21:13.711 --> 00:21:16.190
the reward functions
are even smaller.

00:21:16.191 --> 00:21:18.200
So, there's no way

00:21:18.201 --> 00:21:20.010
we're going to capture
all the subtlety.

00:21:20.011 --> 00:21:22.590
Even humans are not going
to understand the world.

00:21:22.591 --> 00:21:24.260
They have a bunch of heuristics,

00:21:24.261 --> 00:21:25.860
they have a bunch of
ways to compress it,

00:21:25.861 --> 00:21:27.990
but even then it's
not perfect and then,

00:21:27.991 --> 00:21:30.700
if we're crippling things
further by having

00:21:30.701 --> 00:21:33.750
these very hacky proxies for

00:21:33.751 --> 00:21:35.870
reward functions that just seems

00:21:35.871 --> 00:21:39.010
to me like a recipe for
things going wrong.

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:41.800
&gt;&gt; If you could just reflect now,

00:21:41.801 --> 00:21:44.950
I think you spent
time today doing

00:21:44.951 --> 00:21:48.720
your distinguished lecture
talking about

00:21:48.721 --> 00:21:51.220
your current direction
which is to figure out

00:21:51.221 --> 00:21:54.055
just a very shared goal
with Microsoft Research.

00:21:54.056 --> 00:21:57.560
If it's in this realm,

00:21:57.561 --> 00:22:00.390
how to weave human intellect into

00:22:00.391 --> 00:22:03.470
the process of designing
and guiding these systems?

00:22:03.471 --> 00:22:05.270
Say a little bit
more about how that,

00:22:05.271 --> 00:22:08.155
what spark that whole direction
in your work right now.

00:22:08.156 --> 00:22:11.185
&gt;&gt; So, I think

00:22:11.186 --> 00:22:14.990
the original work on that

00:22:14.991 --> 00:22:16.860
about a year and a half now

00:22:16.861 --> 00:22:19.775
was myself and one of
the people in my group,

00:22:19.776 --> 00:22:22.620
Paul Christiano, who
had been writing.

00:22:22.621 --> 00:22:24.080
On one hand Paul had
been writing about

00:22:24.081 --> 00:22:26.740
things similar to
this for a while.

00:22:26.741 --> 00:22:29.210
I got very excited about
from the safety perspective,

00:22:29.211 --> 00:22:32.695
and I think there's also

00:22:32.696 --> 00:22:34.590
a fairly rich academic literature

00:22:34.591 --> 00:22:37.985
on putting humans into
the training loop,

00:22:37.986 --> 00:22:40.480
but a lot of it
hadn't caught up to

00:22:40.481 --> 00:22:42.920
the power of RL systems

00:22:42.921 --> 00:22:45.040
and it hadn't been done
for deep learning.

00:22:45.041 --> 00:22:46.170
We really didn't know if it was

00:22:46.171 --> 00:22:47.440
something that worked at scale.

00:22:47.441 --> 00:22:51.270
So, we said, "Let's see if on

00:22:51.271 --> 00:22:55.719
modern RL tasks and
modern AI systems,

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:57.615
we can make this,

00:22:57.616 --> 00:22:59.760
put humans in the loop
during training

00:22:59.761 --> 00:23:02.430
in order to tell AI systems what

00:23:02.431 --> 00:23:04.440
their goals should be and

00:23:04.441 --> 00:23:05.970
what human values they should

00:23:05.971 --> 00:23:07.640
embody and then at test time,

00:23:07.641 --> 00:23:10.920
the agents can act
completely autonomously."

00:23:10.921 --> 00:23:14.550
This seemed like
a really appealing approach

00:23:14.551 --> 00:23:16.470
because it means
the AI system can

00:23:16.471 --> 00:23:18.505
in the end act
completely autonomously,

00:23:18.506 --> 00:23:20.900
but because it's interactive
with humans during training,

00:23:20.901 --> 00:23:23.135
it embodies a picture of

00:23:23.136 --> 00:23:26.340
human values and human
directives and goals.

00:23:26.341 --> 00:23:29.410
So, we spent a few months
trying to get that to work

00:23:29.411 --> 00:23:33.300
and found that for a lot of
the modern RL task we could,

00:23:33.301 --> 00:23:34.450
in fact get it to work.

00:23:34.451 --> 00:23:35.580
So, a lot of

00:23:35.581 --> 00:23:38.530
our work more recently
has branched off that.

00:23:38.531 --> 00:23:39.950
&gt;&gt; It seems that there's several

00:23:39.951 --> 00:23:41.985
different approaches to that.

00:23:41.986 --> 00:23:44.665
We've done a number of

00:23:44.666 --> 00:23:47.620
projects where we try to
characterize human intellect,

00:23:47.621 --> 00:23:50.840
characterize machine intellect,
understand blindspots,

00:23:50.841 --> 00:23:54.195
understand what each agent

00:23:54.196 --> 00:23:55.750
does a great job
and understand how

00:23:55.751 --> 00:23:57.770
to do the handoffs or

00:23:57.771 --> 00:24:00.750
the synthesis of
these intellection.

00:24:00.751 --> 00:24:03.230
The interesting work you

00:24:03.231 --> 00:24:04.720
just talked about was
about for example,

00:24:04.721 --> 00:24:07.455
humans providing hence feedback,

00:24:07.456 --> 00:24:12.640
shaping of the utility function,
and request reward.

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:15.370
It seems that there are
several different ways to

00:24:15.371 --> 00:24:16.960
go in this fund.

00:24:16.961 --> 00:24:20.460
So, what are some interesting
directions there?

00:24:20.461 --> 00:24:23.860
&gt;&gt; So, as you mentioned
the original paper

00:24:23.861 --> 00:24:27.630
was like agent gives
some examples of,

00:24:27.631 --> 00:24:29.200
what its behavior to

00:24:29.201 --> 00:24:31.695
the human and then ask
which of these are best?

00:24:31.696 --> 00:24:34.645
What is more like, what
you want me to do?

00:24:34.646 --> 00:24:37.400
In the original paper was
like a binary choice.

00:24:37.401 --> 00:24:39.835
It was like is left
better or the right

00:24:39.836 --> 00:24:42.910
better&gt; Now we're expanding
in various directions.

00:24:42.911 --> 00:24:44.810
Thought is, can the human give

00:24:44.811 --> 00:24:47.945
feedback to the AI system
in natural language?

00:24:47.946 --> 00:24:51.600
Or can it give feedback
more indirectly?

00:24:51.601 --> 00:24:54.050
Can one AI system help a

00:24:54.051 --> 00:24:57.465
human understand how to
train another AI system?

00:24:57.466 --> 00:25:00.735
So, that's a more
complicated way of having

00:25:00.736 --> 00:25:02.750
humans and existing AIs work

00:25:02.751 --> 00:25:05.280
together to train
more powerful AIs.

00:25:05.281 --> 00:25:08.455
We're also thinking of

00:25:08.456 --> 00:25:12.855
going into more like
language focus direction.

00:25:12.856 --> 00:25:15.730
We're thinking of combining
it with other techniques.

00:25:15.731 --> 00:25:17.760
The common technique in
reinforcement learning

00:25:17.761 --> 00:25:21.000
is imitation learning where human

00:25:21.001 --> 00:25:24.540
demonstrates a task a bunch
of times and AI system

00:25:24.541 --> 00:25:26.095
looks at that and tries to

00:25:26.096 --> 00:25:28.280
copy it or learn what
the human is doing.

00:25:28.281 --> 00:25:31.130
So, that's another way
to get humans involved.

00:25:31.131 --> 00:25:33.400
I think our general vision is,

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:36.350
let's work to try
and find ways to

00:25:36.351 --> 00:25:38.530
combine all of
these features together.

00:25:38.531 --> 00:25:40.680
Because it's a little
how children learn,

00:25:40.681 --> 00:25:42.555
they learn by copying adults,

00:25:42.556 --> 00:25:44.020
they learn by getting feedback on

00:25:44.021 --> 00:25:45.730
whether what they're
doing is good or not,

00:25:45.731 --> 00:25:47.560
sometimes they just know
the answer and they learn

00:25:47.561 --> 00:25:49.610
from that and it's not
really any one thing,

00:25:49.611 --> 00:25:52.020
it's a mix of all those things.

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:55.525
&gt;&gt; What is some your thoughts on

00:25:55.526 --> 00:25:57.000
applying these methods someday to

00:25:57.001 --> 00:26:01.400
build a system that has

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:06.560
the ethical insights and

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:10.750
wisdom that we might expect

00:26:10.751 --> 00:26:13.110
from a sage we look

00:26:13.111 --> 00:26:15.745
up to when it comes
to hard decisions.

00:26:15.746 --> 00:26:17.075
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I mean that's

00:26:17.076 --> 00:26:20.300
the ultimate ambition
of this sort of thing.

00:26:20.301 --> 00:26:22.170
I mean, obviously
there's a lot of

00:26:22.171 --> 00:26:26.125
things between what we
can do now and that,

00:26:26.126 --> 00:26:28.670
just as there's a lot
of things between

00:26:28.671 --> 00:26:31.440
today's AI systems and AI systems

00:26:31.441 --> 00:26:34.350
that are as good as
humans and everything.

00:26:34.351 --> 00:26:40.710
But I think we're trying
to gradually scale up,

00:26:40.711 --> 00:26:43.810
introduce new methods,
make progress by pieces.

00:26:43.811 --> 00:26:46.370
I think one feature that
will be really important

00:26:46.371 --> 00:26:50.605
is in using humans
to train AI systems,

00:26:50.606 --> 00:26:53.070
there are number of
biases that humans

00:26:53.071 --> 00:26:55.200
have and a number of

00:26:55.201 --> 00:26:57.860
limitations in
humans understanding

00:26:57.861 --> 00:26:59.170
their own values and being able

00:26:59.171 --> 00:27:00.845
to impart their own values.

00:27:00.846 --> 00:27:03.440
So, we're actively looking for

00:27:03.441 --> 00:27:08.100
collaboration with
social scientists,

00:27:08.101 --> 00:27:09.509
behavioral psychologists,

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:11.975
cognitive scientists, economists,

00:27:11.976 --> 00:27:14.580
those types of people to

00:27:14.581 --> 00:27:17.110
help us to design
those experiment.

00:27:17.111 --> 00:27:19.850
We think this isn't purely
a technical problem,

00:27:19.851 --> 00:27:23.165
it has like a social
science component to it.

00:27:23.166 --> 00:27:25.970
As well, we're hoping
increasingly as we move to

00:27:25.971 --> 00:27:30.170
harder tasks to get
social scientists more involved.

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:33.595
&gt;&gt; I get the sense that you're
an optimist overall about

00:27:33.596 --> 00:27:36.830
where technology is
going, is that true?

00:27:36.831 --> 00:27:40.085
&gt;&gt; I mean,

00:27:40.086 --> 00:27:42.905
I think I'm an optimist overall,

00:27:42.906 --> 00:27:46.030
in the sense that
I think if we're

00:27:46.031 --> 00:27:51.840
thoughtful about how
to use technology,

00:27:51.841 --> 00:27:54.810
that if we think
really hard about,

00:27:54.811 --> 00:27:57.410
it is possible to
get a good outcome.

00:27:57.411 --> 00:28:01.340
At the same, I hesitated
a little because I think

00:28:01.341 --> 00:28:03.100
it really depends in us and it

00:28:03.101 --> 00:28:04.970
depends on how things are used.

00:28:04.971 --> 00:28:07.180
Right, if I look at the way a lot

00:28:07.181 --> 00:28:10.035
of social media stuff
was rolled out,

00:28:10.036 --> 00:28:12.300
and just the effects it's had

00:28:12.301 --> 00:28:14.960
on democracy and elections over

00:28:14.961 --> 00:28:17.770
the last few years like we
didn't think about any of

00:28:17.771 --> 00:28:20.920
that stuff ahead of
time and as a result,

00:28:20.921 --> 00:28:24.265
while these technologies
have had positive impacts,

00:28:24.266 --> 00:28:26.680
we're starting to see
their negative side

00:28:26.681 --> 00:28:28.315
as well, and so,

00:28:28.316 --> 00:28:32.785
it's hard to be
like a starry-eyed,

00:28:32.786 --> 00:28:38.085
unmitigated optimist when
you see that all around you.

00:28:38.086 --> 00:28:40.610
But, the lesson I
take from it is that,

00:28:40.611 --> 00:28:42.985
with AI we shouldn't
let that happen,

00:28:42.986 --> 00:28:44.745
we should instead think
about everything,

00:28:44.746 --> 00:28:47.615
we should think about
the downsides ahead of time,

00:28:47.616 --> 00:28:49.600
we shouldn't have a culture where

00:28:49.601 --> 00:28:53.905
we attack people for thinking
about the downsides.

00:28:53.906 --> 00:28:56.840
It's like an important
function that, I think,

00:28:56.841 --> 00:28:59.030
is actually core to actually

00:28:59.031 --> 00:29:01.495
getting the technology to work,

00:29:01.496 --> 00:29:03.580
actually getting it to
deliver its benefits,

00:29:03.581 --> 00:29:05.290
and that if we don't have

00:29:05.291 --> 00:29:07.325
these thoughts and if we don't
have these conversations,

00:29:07.326 --> 00:29:09.425
then there's going
to be this backlash.

00:29:09.426 --> 00:29:14.570
&gt;&gt; So, let's push on
optimism for a second.

00:29:14.571 --> 00:29:19.270
Just speculate with me
if 15-20 years from now,

00:29:19.271 --> 00:29:21.280
what do we see, where are we with

00:29:21.281 --> 00:29:23.805
AI in terms of
applications, theory?

00:29:23.806 --> 00:29:25.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah. i mean, I think

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:28.990
in terms of applications, I mean,

00:29:28.991 --> 00:29:33.605
you mentioned we both

00:29:33.606 --> 00:29:35.220
share this background
of having thought

00:29:35.221 --> 00:29:41.720
about neurobiology and
just the structure

00:29:41.721 --> 00:29:44.895
of the brain and the structure
of biochemistry,

00:29:44.896 --> 00:29:46.615
you really get this sense

00:29:46.616 --> 00:29:48.435
looking at the
problems of biology,

00:29:48.436 --> 00:29:51.120
that they're beyond
human scale, right.

00:29:51.121 --> 00:29:57.570
You look at these charts of
metabolism, every cell has.

00:29:57.571 --> 00:29:59.340
&gt;&gt; Don't remind me of having
to memorize all that stuff.

00:29:59.341 --> 00:30:02.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah. It's terrible.

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:05.070
You know, every cell has

00:30:05.071 --> 00:30:07.460
7,000 different kinds
of proteins that get

00:30:07.461 --> 00:30:10.120
expressed out of
30,000 or so genes

00:30:10.121 --> 00:30:12.880
and some proteins have one copy,

00:30:12.881 --> 00:30:14.395
some half a million copy,

00:30:14.396 --> 00:30:16.300
some of them are
like phosphorylated,

00:30:16.301 --> 00:30:17.850
some of them aren't, and it's

00:30:17.851 --> 00:30:19.360
just no human can remember it.

00:30:19.361 --> 00:30:20.740
There's all this data, we're

00:30:20.741 --> 00:30:22.230
using machine
learning a little bit

00:30:22.231 --> 00:30:26.620
to analyze pieces of
the data but really,

00:30:26.621 --> 00:30:27.940
it seems to me

00:30:27.941 --> 00:30:31.890
that this is
almost a problem tailor

00:30:31.891 --> 00:30:34.655
built for machines, and so,

00:30:34.656 --> 00:30:37.885
I'm hopeful and I've
heard other people,

00:30:37.886 --> 00:30:40.330
even at Microsoft saying

00:30:40.331 --> 00:30:41.840
the same thing and

00:30:41.841 --> 00:30:43.980
I think it's a correct
optimistic vision.

00:30:43.981 --> 00:30:46.585
I really hope that we can

00:30:46.586 --> 00:30:50.840
use AI to take over more
of a scientific process,

00:30:50.841 --> 00:30:54.965
maybe someday all of
the scientific process and

00:30:54.966 --> 00:30:58.570
cure long standing diseases

00:30:58.571 --> 00:31:00.410
like cancer that just
have this complexity,

00:31:00.411 --> 00:31:02.960
that we have a hard time
reckoning with.

00:31:02.961 --> 00:31:06.240
Similarly, for things like

00:31:06.241 --> 00:31:09.630
global warming or
energy problems,

00:31:09.631 --> 00:31:11.760
just technology
problems in general

00:31:11.761 --> 00:31:16.495
which all told humans aren't
really that great at.

00:31:16.496 --> 00:31:18.455
&gt;&gt; So, that's 25 years from now.

00:31:18.456 --> 00:31:19.275
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:19.276 --> 00:31:21.555
&gt;&gt; Something costly
has happened that

00:31:21.556 --> 00:31:24.165
people say that was
a problem with AI.

00:31:24.166 --> 00:31:24.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:24.691 --> 00:31:26.705
&gt;&gt; What's your best guess

00:31:26.706 --> 00:31:29.615
what had happened over
that period of time?

00:31:29.616 --> 00:31:31.125
&gt;&gt; I mean, there's lots of

00:31:31.126 --> 00:31:33.565
kinds of things that
could go wrong,

00:31:33.566 --> 00:31:35.725
I mean, I think,

00:31:35.726 --> 00:31:38.590
when I think about
the accident stuff.

00:31:38.591 --> 00:31:42.010
Right, there's
the most extreme scenario

00:31:42.011 --> 00:31:43.560
is the world gets destroyed,

00:31:43.561 --> 00:31:45.430
and I think that there are

00:31:45.431 --> 00:31:47.330
scenarios where
that could happen.

00:31:47.331 --> 00:31:49.450
For instance, if
you had AI systems

00:31:49.451 --> 00:31:51.920
managing nuclear weapons
or something like that,

00:31:51.921 --> 00:31:53.530
for a country turned over it's

00:31:53.531 --> 00:31:56.480
national defense to AI or
something like that and the

00:31:56.481 --> 00:32:00.860
in the AI wasn't well-designed
in the sense that

00:32:00.861 --> 00:32:05.630
it didn't even do what
it was designed to do,

00:32:05.631 --> 00:32:09.820
systems that are supposed
to manage the economy.

00:32:09.821 --> 00:32:12.280
If we really get to
the point where we're

00:32:12.281 --> 00:32:14.790
really putting the whole
world's trust in AI systems,

00:32:14.791 --> 00:32:15.930
then I do think there are

00:32:15.931 --> 00:32:18.240
extreme outcomes
that could happen.

00:32:18.241 --> 00:32:19.980
On the less extreme side,

00:32:19.981 --> 00:32:25.160
just things like
economic crashes,

00:32:25.161 --> 00:32:29.220
systems being unsafe causing

00:32:30.300 --> 00:32:32.650
individual casualties on

00:32:32.651 --> 00:32:37.275
the social misuse and
responsibility side.

00:32:37.276 --> 00:32:44.300
I think the potential invasiveness
of AI technology could

00:32:44.301 --> 00:32:47.760
have a lot of concerns

00:32:47.761 --> 00:32:52.230
related to pervasive surveillance
and things like that,

00:32:52.231 --> 00:32:57.320
just misuse of AI in general

00:32:57.321 --> 00:33:00.215
by nation states in

00:33:00.216 --> 00:33:03.015
changing the world
role balance of power.

00:33:03.016 --> 00:33:07.690
&gt;&gt; So, turning from your
thoughts about the future to

00:33:07.691 --> 00:33:11.170
maybe some current questions

00:33:11.171 --> 00:33:13.710
from some folks watching this,

00:33:13.711 --> 00:33:15.520
especially with regards
to career you've had

00:33:15.521 --> 00:33:18.130
such an illustrious career even,

00:33:18.131 --> 00:33:21.830
given how early you
are in your career,

00:33:21.831 --> 00:33:23.840
going through many different
phases of interests,

00:33:23.841 --> 00:33:28.430
following the natural terrain
of your curiosity,

00:33:28.431 --> 00:33:31.830
I'm curious what your advice
might be to folks thinking

00:33:31.831 --> 00:33:36.565
about their major in college
or graduate work and beyond?

00:33:36.566 --> 00:33:38.770
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, I mean
I definitely think

00:33:38.771 --> 00:33:43.300
that AI machine
learning is going to

00:33:43.301 --> 00:33:48.095
continue to be just a big player

00:33:48.096 --> 00:33:49.950
in the general advance
of technology.

00:33:49.951 --> 00:33:54.300
I mean, even if we stopped
all research advances today,

00:33:54.301 --> 00:33:56.240
just the pure applications

00:33:56.241 --> 00:33:57.670
of what we already have

00:33:57.671 --> 00:33:59.275
would be economically
transformative,

00:33:59.276 --> 00:34:01.500
and I don't think that
the research advances are going

00:34:01.501 --> 00:34:04.555
to ground to a standstill.

00:34:04.556 --> 00:34:09.730
So, I think it's a very
promising career direction.

00:34:09.731 --> 00:34:11.580
I really encourage people

00:34:11.581 --> 00:34:15.535
to think about
the social implications,

00:34:15.536 --> 00:34:18.790
just the number of undergrads

00:34:18.791 --> 00:34:20.200
and young people do

00:34:20.201 --> 00:34:21.730
seem to already be
thinking about that.

00:34:21.731 --> 00:34:23.205
So, it seems like people are

00:34:23.206 --> 00:34:25.460
thinking in the right direction.

00:34:25.461 --> 00:34:30.910
I've just met a lot of
people who seem like they

00:34:30.911 --> 00:34:33.380
just genuinely care
about both advancing

00:34:33.381 --> 00:34:34.755
the technology and
making sure that

00:34:34.756 --> 00:34:37.055
it's good for the world.

00:34:37.056 --> 00:34:39.545
One piece of advice I have is,

00:34:39.546 --> 00:34:43.460
I really think ML and
Al and are becoming

00:34:43.461 --> 00:34:48.640
much more hands on
fields and instead

00:34:48.641 --> 00:34:54.940
of waiting to take
grad school courses in them or

00:34:54.941 --> 00:34:58.040
that sort of thing that just

00:34:58.041 --> 00:35:00.840
like checking out GitHub repos

00:35:00.841 --> 00:35:03.010
for like all the latest models,

00:35:03.011 --> 00:35:04.390
trying to implement
them yourself,

00:35:04.391 --> 00:35:06.500
trying to tinker with
them yourself, I mean,

00:35:06.501 --> 00:35:11.700
I think it started as just as
like programming used to be

00:35:11.701 --> 00:35:14.450
academic computer science
and then you move

00:35:14.451 --> 00:35:17.260
to become more of a
practical discipline,

00:35:17.261 --> 00:35:19.620
I think AI or many parts of

00:35:19.621 --> 00:35:22.645
AI are much more moving
to be this kind of

00:35:22.646 --> 00:35:24.980
practical discipline that it's

00:35:24.981 --> 00:35:26.670
about your ability
to pattern match

00:35:26.671 --> 00:35:30.550
and so you want to get cycles
of iteration in very early,

00:35:30.551 --> 00:35:31.980
you want to implement
lots of stuff,

00:35:31.981 --> 00:35:34.640
you want to know
models back and forth.

00:35:34.641 --> 00:35:36.545
You also need to know the theory

00:35:36.546 --> 00:35:42.380
but there's less of that
than there used to be,

00:35:42.381 --> 00:35:44.910
and a lot of it is
starting to consolidate.

00:35:44.911 --> 00:35:47.020
&gt;&gt; Well, great. Well, it is

00:35:47.021 --> 00:35:49.060
fabulous talking to you today.
Thanks very much, Dario.

00:35:49.061 --> 00:35:50.990
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me.

